d against you  or be arrested  for that crime for leaving your child in  or pet in that hot vehicle  it gets right back to the if you see  something say something mantra that i  know a lot of law enforcement uses  let's switch gears sir let's talk about  something that we all saw  last week and that was a video from law  enforcement of terrell rhodes  the suspect in that amari nicholson that  horrible case of the two-year-old who  died  rhodes the video shows he grabbed a gun  from detectives  in the interview room during the process  how does something like that happen  especially when a murder suspect's in  custody that was a dangerous situation  yeah uh you know that was a horrible  case all around for  the family for our detectives and for  the community  uh we we you know in that situation you  know  to address it exactly what had happened  was our officers had been interviewing  the suspect roads  and during that interview they had  stepped outside  those rooms are under video surveillance  at all times  the officers are within policy to have  their guns in that interview room  but they observed the suspect roads uh  messing with the handcuffs  while they were outside of that room  when they went in to confront him as  you're watching the video  you saw what occurred he was able to  pull one of the officer's guns  out of the holster and our officers were  able to quickly take that situation  under control  and and we were able to have a have a  desirable outcome instead of a deadly  force outcome  there also we train for that  we have our close quarter type scenarios  we we do firearms training we do  defensive tactics training  but in an incident like this our  critical incident review team  we'll take a look at that review it look  at things  look at our policies look at our  procedures look at things we can do  better  because we always like to come out of  these things with better training and  better tools for our officers  when they deal with situations 